  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, it was cloudy, except over the southeast corner of the \nCWA where it was sunny. The cold front that began entering the \nnorthwest corner of the CWA yesterday afternoon has finally made it \nto the southeast corner of the CWA. Temperatures under the clouds \nbehind the cold front are only in the 50s and 60s, some 15 to 25+ \ndegrees colder than this time yesterday. Where it's been sunny, \ntemperatures have warmed into the upper 60s to mid 70s. Winds are \nfrom the north generally 10 to 20 mph with some occasionally higher \ngusts. Along and south of the cold frontal boundary, winds are light \nsoutherly (less than 10 mph) or light and variable.\n\nAs ensemble clusters analysis and deterministic model solutions work \nto close the gap on range of potential outcomes beyond the Wednesday \ndaytime forecast period, it would appear the latest iteration of \nmodels may be trying to take a portion of Rex Block closed low off \nto the east with energy working down the back of the western CONUS \nupper level ridge, while retrograding a portion of that closed low \nsouthwestward toward the southwestern CONUS. By Sunday (day 7), \nmodels forecast the amplified pattern to de-amplify into a nearly \nzonal flow.\n\nStill looking at pretty minimal precipitation amounts potential \ntonight through Tuesday morning while southwest flow aloft is able \nto draw a few transient shortwaves over the region. Upper level col \nregion is in place Tuesday/Tuesday night, so not much of a chance \nfor precipitation at that time. Wednesday/night could see some of \nthe deformation/WAA zone precipitation potential from this Rex Block \nclosed low over the intermountain west extending up into the CWA. \nOtherwise, most of the rest of the 7-day forecast is dry.\n\nTemperatures, for the most part, are not going to move much off \ntheir current diurnal range (lows in the 30s/40s and highs in the \n50s/60s) with low level thermal advection remaining rather static \nduring a lot of the 7-day forecast period.",
